    key: "S6-020",
    domainCode: 6,
    stem: `An all-purpose cluster that hosts the team's interactive notebooks was restarted overnight: sessions died, and the uptime counter had reset by morning. The engineer did not schedule any restart and wants to reconstruct exactly what happened to the compute while nobody was watching — whether it was resized, terminated, or failed, and at what time. Where is that record kept?`,
    options: [
      "In the job run history, which records every cluster lifecycle event — creations, resizes, terminations — of the compute that jobs used",
      "In the cluster event log, which is written only for job clusters and never for all-purpose compute",
      "In the cluster event log, which records timestamped events such as creation, resizes, terminations, and failures",
      "In no view at all, because the platform does not persist cluster-level events like restarts or resizes",
    ],
    correctIndex: 2,
    explanationEn: `Every compute keeps an event log (the Event log tab on the cluster's page): a chronological, timestamped record of lifecycle events — cluster creation, upscaling and downscaling, terminations with their reason and initiator, and failures such as init script errors or cloud capacity problems. That is the record for reconstructing the overnight restart. The job run history tracks job tasks and their states, not cluster lifecycle, and no job was even running overnight. The event log is not exclusive to job clusters — all-purpose clusters have one as well. And these events are persisted by the platform, not lost.`,
    explanationPt: `Todo compute mantém um event log (a aba Event log na página do cluster): um registro cronológico, com timestamps, dos eventos de ciclo de vida — criação do cluster, upscaling e downscaling, terminações com motivo e iniciador, e falhas como erros de init script ou problemas de capacidade na nuvem. É esse o registro para reconstruir a reinicialização noturna. O run history do job acompanha tasks e estados do job, não o ciclo de vida do cluster — e nenhum job sequer rodou de madrugada. O event log não é exclusivo de job clusters: all-purpose clusters também têm. E esses eventos ficam persistidos pela plataforma, não se perdem.`,
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure",
    referenceTitle: "Configure clusters — Databricks Docs",
